= Hello!

After banging my head against the wall for a weekend teaching myself some rudimentary web development I've now finally got a blog!
I think blogging is a bit of a lost art and if you know me you know I love outdated tech and doing things the manual way, so why not try host one on my own wesbsite?

I'm yet to decide what I'll actually write on here but my main goal is to get away from the algorithms and feeds of Instagram and Reddit and take a more deliberate approach to what I post and share with my friends.

= Why go through all the effort?

Cause I'm sick of social media!

Why do I have to use a gross social media app like Instagram just to post photos for my friends to see? I want to have control over my own life and control my data online.
By running my own blog I have full control over my data and none of it gets sold off by advertisers to track what I do and try sell me things I don't want.
My inspiration for this came from a few excellent creators that I've been watching and my frustration with how much time I waste slop scrolling on my phone.

= My inspirations

The two main big catalysts for me wanting to get away from algorithmic feeds was #link("https://rejectconvenience.com/")[Reject Convenience] and #link("https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QEJpZjg8GuA")[this video] from #link("https://www.youtube.com/@TechnologyConnections")[Technology Connections]

== Reject Convenience
Anthony is an amazing up and coming creator who talks about privacy and technoogy, what I love about his videos is that he doesn't fearmonger and sensationalise online privacy --- he always focuses on the importance of being informed and making your own decisions about what you feel comfortable sharing.

His video #link("https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yGlXEcLPyyI")[What if you can't reject convenience?] is a great introduction video to his channel and I really appreciate how much he stresses not making drastic changes to your life that you're not comfortable with.

== Technology Connections

Alec **usually** makes videos about usually overlooked elements of the technology thats all around us (plus an annual video complaining about modern christmas lights), but his video #link("https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QEJpZjg8GuA")[Algorithms are breaking the way we think] really opened my eyes to how much of what I was consuming wasn't actually chosen by me.

I really agree with him that it's worth curating what you view online and don't just rely on what the algorithms give you, I think we should all be more deliberate in the things we do and avoid letting big tech companies decide for us.

= But how can I follow a blog?

Wouldn't it be great if instead of needing to post on an app everyone had their own site? but then that would be too much hassle to remember to check everyone's websites for new posts... Except this is a solved problem!
I realise I'm preaching to the choir with a lot of my techy friends but so many people don't know that there is an open standard for sharing feeds over the internet and it's called #link("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RSS")[RSS.]

Using a feed agregator app --- I personally use Akregator from KDE --- you can subscribe to feeds (like mine!). You'd be surprised how many sites have it that you never realised! I use it to follow my favourite webcomics now that I don't use instagram anymore.

= So what are you going to post about?

I don't really know yet!

I'm aiming to replace any other social media so I'll probably post mostly life updates and share my photography, if I get a sudden inspiration for some serious editorial work then I'll post articles like that too.
I don't really care if anyone ever reads these since that's not why I made this.

Ultimately I just want to have an outlet to post about my life for my friends to be able to see and read, and remind people that you don't have to let a bunch of billionares in another country decide what you read and watch online.